79906660:
Certainly you should prefer the actual global module fragment over the emulation thereof, for several reasons: 

 #include of certain header files may get transformed into import , which must appear immediately after the module declaration (or else in the global module fragment). 
 If an #include is not so translated, the file included might contain an import which would be so restricted. 
 Declarations in the global module fragment are discarded if they are not directly used, so that they do not require space in the compiled module interface and time in importers. (Obviously you might want to provide access to more of the interface, but that would involve export using declarations that would prevent the discarding anyway.) 
 Build systems try to optimize scanning module files to find dependencies, and #include s later would defeat that. 


